sequenceDiagram
    actor User as CLI User
    participant RC as RunCommand
    participant Sub as OS Subprocess
    participant S3 as AWS S3
    participant SSM as AWS SSM
    participant EC2 as AWS EC2
    participant CF as AWS CloudFormation
    participant DDB as DynamoDB results table
    participant GH as GitHub Releases (laptop only)

    User->>RC: baas run <type> [params] --benchmark-jar <path> [--project] [--instance-type] [--timeout]<br/>[--watchdog-margin] [--runner-jar] [--tag k=v] [--format json] [--config-path]
    Note over RC: validate type in {jmh, jmh-with-async, jmh-with-prof, jcstress}<br/>ConfigService.load() from --config-path, else ~/.baas/config.yaml
    Note over RC: an unreleased CLI (0.0.0-semantically-released) names no<br/>release to pin the runner JAR to, so it exits 1 naming<br/>--runner-jar -- before any upload. baas run builds nothing.
    opt no --project and git.resolveProject enabled
        RC->>Sub: git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir (in the JAR's directory)
        Sub-->>RC: repo root -> project name, or "not a git repository"
    end
    Note over RC: resolve project AND results table BEFORE any AWS call.<br/>No --project and nothing derived -> exit 1 naming --project.<br/>No installation in config -> exit 1 naming `baas config sync`.<br/>No option discards measurements: every run records its status<br/>in the results table, so every run needs one.<br/>branch/commit are never derived: only --tag supplies them.<br/>watchdog bound = timeout + margin (margin >= 60).
    Note over RC: the --benchmark-jar option is required, and checked for existence here --<br/>before the runner-image lookup and before any upload.<br/>Missing -> exit 1 naming --benchmark-jar. Nothing is built.
    RC->>SSM: ssm.getParameter(/<prefix>/runner/ami-id)
    SSM-->>RC: amiId, or ParameterNotFound
    RC->>EC2: ec2.describeImages(amiId)
    EC2-->>RC: image + tags (baas-image-version), or InvalidAMIID.NotFound
    Note over RC: no image -> exit 1 naming `baas admin build-image`.<br/>Checked before any upload, so a missing image costs two API<br/>calls rather than a wasted upload. There is no fallback to<br/>AL2023 + yum.
    Note over RC: ONE clock read: runInstant = Instant.now().<br/>runId = RunId.generate(runInstant) -- 20260820T174432812Z-a3f9c21b<br/>resultPath = RunLayout.runPrefix(project, runId)<br/>= runs/<project>/<runId>.<br/>The same instant travels on as --created-at, so the prefix<br/>name and the stored createdAt cannot disagree.
    RC->>CF: describeStacks(prefix) -- SubnetId, SecurityGroupId, BEFORE any upload<br/>(a missing installation fails here, having uploaded nothing)
    RC->>S3: s3.putObject(bucket, runs/<project>/<runId>/input/benchmark.jar)
    S3-->>RC: PutObjectResponse
    alt --runner-jar given
        RC->>S3: s3.putObject(bucket, runs/<project>/<runId>/input/runner.jar)
    else pinned to this CLI's own version
        RC->>S3: headObject(releases/<version>/benchmark-runner.jar)
        opt absent
            RC->>GH: GET releases/download/v<version>/benchmark-runner.jar + .sha256
            GH-->>RC: jar + published checksum
            Note over RC: verify, then putObject. A mismatch uploads<br/>nothing and launches nothing. Never overwrites a<br/>present object, so a corrupt one needs deleting.
        end
    end
    Note over RC: buildRunnerTags(): project, commit, branch, type, source + every --tag.<br/>A --tag naming a machine-observed key (imageVersion,<br/>instanceType, jdk, cpuModel, cpuArch, type) is REJECTED --<br/>those come from the instance, so a result's tags can never<br/>disagree with its own environment.json.<br/>source is derived (ci when CI/GITHUB_ACTIONS is set, else local)<br/>but NOT reserved: how a run was triggered is not observed on<br/>the instance, so --tag source=nightly wins instead of failing.
    Note over RC: UserDataScriptBuilder.build() -- installs NOTHING.<br/>The toolchain is baked into the AMI.<br/>Script captures environment.json + packages.txt and uploads<br/>them BEFORE the benchmark, so a crash still leaves a record,<br/>copies benchmark-runner.jar from the bucket -- the instance<br/>reaches NO host outside the account,<br/>carries the results table name in user-data (no credential,<br/>so no SSM round trip -- RunnerRole grants the access),<br/>defines run_status() BEFORE the watchdog forks (a subshell sees only<br/>functions defined before it), records running after the watchdog --<br/>a refused running write (run already cancelled) ships the boot log<br/>and self-terminates WITHOUT starting the benchmark,<br/>runs: timeout N java -jar benchmark-runner.jar type<br/>--results-table T --tag ... <br/>records completed or failed:N on the run item (the watchdog records timed-out)<br/>using RUN_SORT_KEY as built by the CLI, never rebuilt in the shell<br/>self-terminates the EC2 instance
    RC->>DDB: UpdateItem pk=RUN sk=createdAt#runId -- status launching + identity + CLI-side tags<br/>condition attribute_not_exists(pk)
    Note over RC: reservation fails -> exit 1, NOTHING is launched.<br/>An instance without a record is the invisible run `baas runs` exists to show.
    Note over RC: register JVM shutdown hook NOW, before RunInstances: on Ctrl+C it records<br/>cancelled (5 s timeout) and terminates -- the instance id, or, if RunInstances<br/>has not returned yet, whatever instance carries this run's baas-request-id tag.<br/>That lookup can miss: the instance then finds its running write refused<br/>and terminates itself without starting the benchmark.<br/>Does nothing once the run has an outcome (the instance terminates itself).
    RC->>EC2: ec2.runInstances(amiId, instanceType, subnetId, sgId, instanceProfile, userData, runId)
    Note over EC2: min=1 max=1<br/>shutdownBehavior=TERMINATE<br/>IMDSv2 required<br/>tags: ONLY project=baas, baas-role=benchmark-runner,<br/>baas-request-id=runId -- no caller --tag ever reaches the instance
    alt RunInstances fails
        RC->>DDB: UpdateItem status launch-failed + AWS error code (best effort)
        RC->>S3: putObject(runs/<project>/<runId>/launch-error.txt) (best effort)
        RC-->>User: the launch error, exit 1 -- `baas download <runId>` fetches launch-error.txt
    end
    EC2-->>RC: instanceId
    RC->>DDB: UpdateItem status launched + instanceId -- only while still launching (best effort)
    Note over RC: refused because the run is already terminal (cancelled while launching)<br/>-> terminate the instance just launched, exit 1
    loop poll every 15 seconds
        RC->>DDB: GetItem(pk=RUN, sk) ConsistentRead
        DDB-->>RC: launched, running, or a terminal status
        RC->>EC2: describeInstances(instanceId) -- state
        Note over RC: instance gone with no terminal status -> re-read once, then<br/>measurements stored ? "final status lost" : vanished -- both exit 1.<br/>Poll cap (= watchdog bound) -> record timed-out, terminate, exit 1.
        alt interactive terminal and no --format json
            RC-->>User: status line on STDOUT, redrawn in place:<br/>state · elapsed · instanceId (log lines scroll above it)
        else redirected, piped, CI or --format json
            RC-->>User: log line "Still running (state)... elapsed: Ns" on stderr, as before
        end
    end
    Note over RC: completed -> show results. failed:N, timed-out, cancelled -> exit 1.<br/>A cancelled or timed-out written elsewhere (baas runs terminate)<br/>also terminates a still-live instance.<br/>A benchmark that already ran is never reported as failed<br/>because the summary could not be fetched.
    RC->>DDB: Query on requestId-index (gsi1pk = runId), filter attribute_exists(kind)
    Note over DDB: NO exclusion filter on this path -- naming one run by its id is a<br/>request for THAT run. The project sweep still drops<br/>exclude_from_results=true rows server-side.
    DDB-->>RC: one item per measurement
    RC-->>User: print BENCHMARK | REQUEST_ID | TYPE | MODE | SCORE | ERROR | UNIT and exit 0<br/>naming the runId, which is what `baas download <runId>` takes
    opt --format json
        RC-->>User: one object on STDOUT (diagnostics stay on the logger/stderr):<br/>{runId, project, resultPath, status, exitCode, instanceId}
    end
    Note over RC,User: The summary prints on the FAILURE path too, with status "failed",<br/>while the command still exits non-zero -- a failed run is precisely<br/>when CI needs the id, to baas download it and surface<br/>cloud-init-output.log. A run that died before being named reports<br/>null fields rather than no output at all.
